title: Cerebral Abscess
---

# Cerebral Abscess

- **Definition** - suppurative infection of the cerebral parenchyma acquired from a different foci, subsequently progression into abscess formation
- **Pathophysiology of cerebral abscess:**
  - **Access of bacterial into cerebral substances** - the source of infection <u>informs the likely pathogens</u>, which guides Tx (see below)
  - **Local suppurative process** - initial infection results in a local suppurative process and pus formation, leading to an inflammatory response
  - **Organisation into abscess** - local damage results in <u>activation of astrocytes and gliossis to contain the infection</u>, which results in loculation of pus within the wall of gliosis, subsequently resulting in a tough abscess
- **Source of infection:** 
![](../img/source_of_infection_in_cerebral_abscesses.png)
- **Clinical features of cerebral abscess:**
  - <u>Fever</u> - may present acutely as fever suggestive an infective process
  - <u>Features of raised ICP</u> - may present as headache, HV, meningism, and drowsiness
  - <u>Focal neurological S/S</u> - localising neurological S/S may present acutely, but may also present over days or weeks w/ no evidence of infection (distinction from cerebral tumour impossible on clinical grounds)
  - <u>Seizures</u> - progression into epilepsy is frequent and is often resistant to Tx
- **Ix:**
  - **Routine bloods** - CBC, ESR:
    - <u>CBC</u> - shows leukocytosis reflecting active infection
    - <u>ESR</u> - non-specifically elevated
  - **CT** - diagnostic and is often indicated w/ features of raised ICP and focal neurological S/S where LP is contraindicated:
    - Hypodense lesion with rim enhancement
    - Associated surrounding hypodense cerebral (vasogenic) oedema
    - Midline shift present if sizable abscess

    
    ![](../img/contrast_ct_of_cerebral_abscess.png)
  - **LP** - potentially hazardous in presence of raised ICP and may be contraindicated
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>ABx therapy</u> - source of infection guides choice of ABx (vancomycin added in neurosurgical patients)
    - <u>Surgical Mx</u> - burr-hole aspiration or excision and drainage of abscess may be necessary
